speechtext:
The
RCMP Missing Children Registry (MCR) in Ottawa was established by the federal Government to assist all police departments in recovering missing children. Since July 1986, it has ensured that missing children reports from across Canada are entered immediately into the Canadian Police Information Computer (CPIC). The Registry offers the following services:
(i)	co-ordinates inquiries from small Canadian police departments regarding abductions;
(ii)	provides an interface with all Canadian police agencies and private organizations concerned with missing children, such as Child Find, Victims of Violence, and the Tania Murrell Society;
(iii)	acts as a clearing house for Canada in connection with missing children cases when dealing with U.S. and international police departments, state clearing houses, and organizations such as the U.S. National Centre for Missing and Exploited Children;
(iv)	searches Registry files in order to associate unidentified bodies with any missing children reports.
The MCR is also involved in developing search techniques. A representative of the MCR participated in the work of an RCMP committee which has developed a standard technique for urban and rural searches for missing children.
The Report of that committee, entitled Police Procedures for Ground Searches and Rescue for Lost and Missing Persons, has been approved by the Canadian police community and is now undergoing final editing prior to publication and distribution to Canadian police departments.
